cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Independent Monte Carlo Verification of Paleopole Orientation Clustering in the Buildreps Database"
message: "If you use this work, please cite the current version (v3) and the pre-registration; see preferred-citation and references."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Gherbi"
    given-names: "Salah-Eddin"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4017-1095"
    email: "salahealer@gmail.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/salahealer9/paleopole-orientation-verification"
url: "https://github.com/salahealer9/paleopole-orientation-verification"
abstract: >-
  An independent, pre-registered statistical verification of orientation-clustering
  claims associated with the Buildreps database of approximately 1,159 ancient
  pyramids and temples. Version 2 of this report (deposited 1 June 2026) reported
  apparent within-hemisphere clustering at two of the five proposed paleopole
  latitudes — Pole II (76.0°N) and Pole III (72.2°N) — surviving all four
  pre-registered and exploratory null models tested, and characterised that
  clustering as robust. Post-publication review identified two further controls
  that the v2 null models had not applied: a latitude look-elsewhere correction
  (the latitude analogue of the meridian look-elsewhere already in the protocol),
  and a spatial-cluster null that treats nearby structures as one independent
  unit each rather than counting each site as an independent observation. Under
  both controls applied together, no proposed pole shows clustering distinguishable
  from chance. Under the assumption-free conditional null with the latitude
  look-elsewhere correction, the corrected p-values are 0.999 (Pole II) and 0.90
  (Pole III). Under the spatial-cluster null with the same correction, both poles
  return p = 1.0 at every linkage threshold from 25 to 100 km; the 119 site-level
  contributors to the Pole III window reduce to 25 independent clusters at the
  25 km threshold, the largest a single 29-site Mesoamerican cluster in the
  northern Yucatán. Version 3 (current; deposited 5 June 2026) therefore withdraws
  the v2 characterisation of robust clustering at Poles II and III. The pre-registered
  aggregate test statistic, formally significant at p = 0.0001 under the unconditional
  null, was shown by diagnostic analysis to be an artifact of hemisphere-selection
  in the in-range subset; this finding stands unchanged. The corrected conclusion
  is that no proposed pole shows clustering distinguishable from chance. The
  framework's broader interpretive claim — that the observed concentrations
  represent former positions of Earth's rotational axis — is not tested by an
  orientation-clustering analysis and would require independent geological evidence.
